## Docker

{% if choices.docker == "full" -%}
Everything runs in containers; Node.js is not needed on the host. Install dependencies with `docker compose run --rm dev npm ci`, start the dev server with `docker compose up dev` and open http://localhost:{{ ports.WEB_PORT }}. Run every other npm script in the dev container, e.g. `docker compose run --rm dev npm run check` and `docker compose run --rm dev npm run build`. The `dev` service mounts the project, keeps `node_modules` in a named volume and polls for file changes, so hot reload works. `docker compose up --build app` builds and serves the production game; `docker compose --profile dev down` stops everything.
{%- else -%}
Develop on the host with the npm commands above. `docker compose up --build app` builds and serves the production game on http://localhost:{{ ports.WEB_PORT }}; `docker compose build app` only builds the image and `docker compose down` stops it.
{%- endif %}

Both services run as the non-root `node` user. `APP_NAME` and `APP_SLUG` are build arguments, so the production image must be rebuilt after changing them. Saves remain in the player's browser, so no data volume is needed. `docker compose --profile dev down --volumes --remove-orphans --rmi local` removes this project's containers, dependency volume and local images.
{% endif %}